    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A paramount chiefdom of some 30 Algonquian peoples in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wahunsenacah">Wahunsenacah</a>, whom the English called Powhatan.</li><li>It met the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in 1607 and nearly destroyed the colony in 1609–1610 and again in 1622.</li><li>Defeated in 1646, its peoples became tributaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamunkey">Pamunkey</a> and Mattaponi keep reservations confirmed in 1677.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Some of the first Native American words to enter English came from Powhatan, among them raccoon, moccasin, tomahawk and persimmon.</p></aside>
    </section>

